# Grover's Search Algorithm
#
# Finds a marked item in an unsorted database of N items.
# Classical search: O(N) queries on average.
# Grover's algorithm: O(sqrt(N)) queries.
#
# For N=4 (2 qubits), classical needs 2.5 queries on average.
# Grover needs exactly 1 iteration.
#
# The marked item here is |11> (both qubits = 1).
#
# Circuit structure:
#   1. Initialize: H on all qubits (equal superposition)
#   2. Oracle: marks the target state |11> with a phase flip
#   3. Diffusion: amplifies the marked state's amplitude
#   4. Measure: marked state appears with high probability
#
# After 1 Grover iteration on N=4:
# P(finding |11>) ≈ 1.0 (exact for this case)

quantum circuit Grover2:
    qubit q0
    qubit q1

    # Step 1: Equal superposition over all 4 states
    gate hadamard(target=q0)
    gate hadamard(target=q1)

    # Step 2: Oracle — marks |11> with phase flip
    # Implementation: CZ gate via H-CNOT-H
    gate hadamard(target=q1)
    gate cnot(control=q0, target=q1)
    gate hadamard(target=q1)

    # Step 3: Diffusion operator (inversion about average)
    # H on all qubits
    gate hadamard(target=q0)
    gate hadamard(target=q1)
    # Phase flip on |00>
    gate pauli_x(target=q0)
    gate pauli_x(target=q1)
    gate hadamard(target=q1)
    gate cnot(control=q0, target=q1)
    gate hadamard(target=q1)
    gate pauli_x(target=q0)
    gate pauli_x(target=q1)
    # H on all qubits
    gate hadamard(target=q0)
    gate hadamard(target=q1)

    measure q0
    measure q1

infer Grover2 using quantum(shots=1024)

# Expected result: q0=1, q1=1 with ~100% probability
# The marked item |11> is found every time.
